<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1988">1988</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Zia-ul-Haq</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq was a Pakistani army general who overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a> in 1977 and ruled as military ruler and president until his death. He had Bhutto hanged in 1979, pursued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamisation">Islamisation</a> of law and education, and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> the main base for the Afghan mujahideen fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. He died in an unexplained aircraft crash in 1988.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-zia-ul-haq.jpg" alt="Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pakistan Army · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:General_Muhammad_Zia-Ul-Haq.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq was born on 12 August 1924 in Jullundur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, into an Arain family. His father worked at the Army General Headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> and was noted for his religiousness. Zia studied history at St. Stephen's College in Delhi, graduating in 1943, and was commissioned into the British Indian Army, serving in a tank unit, the 13th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">Lancers</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> campaign. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">Partition of India</a> in 1947 he joined the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965">Indo-Pakistani War of 1965</a> as a tank commander, and from 1967 to 1970 headed a Pakistani training mission in Jordan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1975 Zia commanded the II Corps at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, and on 1 March 1976 Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a> made him Chief of Army Staff, choosing the most junior of eight lieutenant generals. Following civil disorder after the 1977 general election, Zia carried out a coup on 5 July 1977, codenamed Operation Fair Play, and Bhutto and his cabinet were arrested. Zia promised elections within 90 days, but they were postponed. The Supreme Court upheld the coup under the doctrine of necessity. Bhutto was arrested again on 3 September 1977 for authorising the murder of a political opponent, and on 18 March 1978 he was sentenced to death. Zia declared himself president on 16 September 1978.</p><p>In 1979 he banned all political parties and disbanded parliament, and at the end of 1981 set up a nominated consultative council, the Majlis-e-Shoora. After the Soviet invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> on 25 December 1979 he decided to support the Afghan mujahideen with American help, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> became the main base of the war against the Soviet forces. He lifted martial law in 1985, after non-partisan elections, but kept wide presidential powers through the Eighth Amendment, and in 1988 dismissed the government of Prime Minister Muhammad Khan Junejo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 17 August 1988 Zia left Bahawalpur, where he had watched a tank demonstration, in a C-130B Hercules. Soon after take-off contact was lost, and witnesses said the plane flew erratically before it crashed. Twenty-nine others died, among them General Akhtar Abdur Rahman and the American ambassador Arnold Lewis Raphel. The cause has given rise to many conspiracy theories. Zia remains a polarising figure: some credit him with checking Soviet advance towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, while critics hold that he weakened democratic institutions and passed laws that encouraged religious intolerance.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zia-ul-Haq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…constitutions of 1956 and 1962 were each swept away by generals, and the 1973 constitution written under Zulfikar Ali Bhutto, which declared an Islamic Republic whose laws must conform to the Quran and Sunnah, was suspended by <b>Zia-ul-Haq</b>'s martial law in 1977 and restored with amendments in 1985.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>General <b>Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq</b>, Pakistan's military ruler for eleven years, died when his C-130 crashed near Bahawalpur.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-17">17 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jalandhar, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Bahawalpur, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.33,75.58&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Jalandhar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6502 1022 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6952 1292h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6952 1292h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.33,75.58&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
